#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# element

#% text
# `element(x, domain)` states that `x` lies in the domain, such as `integers` or `positive_reals`.

#% text
# `element([x, y, ...], domain)` states it for each of the variables.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# With numbers the statement is decided at once and gives `true` or `false`. With symbols it stays as a fact, to be passed to `assuming` or given to `refine`.

#% item
# The domains are `integers`, `rationals`, `reals`, `complexes` and their parts `positive_integers`, `negative_integers`, `nonnegative_integers`, `nonpositive_integers`, `positive_rationals`, `negative_rationals`, `positive_reals`, `negative_reals`, `nonnegative_reals` and `nonpositive_reals`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
element(3, integers)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
element(1/2, integers)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
element(sqrt(2), rationals)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
element(x, reals)
#% output
#= element(x, reals)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several variables at once:

#% cell
assuming(element([x, y], positive_reals), simplify(sqrt(x^2*y^2)))
#% output
#= x*y

#% text
# An integer parameter in trigonometric values:

#% cell
assuming(element(n, integers), refine(cos(2*pi*n)))
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A fact given to `refine` directly:

#% cell
refine(sqrt(x^2), element(x, nonnegative_reals))
#% output
#= x

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The sign of an odd power of a negative integer:

#% cell
assuming(element(k, negative_integers), is_negative(k^5))
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A domain fact and the corresponding inequality act the same way:

#% cell
[assuming(element(x, positive_reals), simplify(sqrt(x^2))), assuming(x > 0, simplify(sqrt(x^2)))]
#% output
#= [x, x]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `assuming`, `refine`, `integers`, `reals`, `positive_reals`
